@{
    ViewData["Title"] = T("Security Whitepaper") + " — DashLane";
    Layout = "_AuthLayout";
}

<div class="legal-page has-toc">
    <aside class="legal-toc" data-toc aria-label="@T("On this page")"><p>@T("On this page")</p><ol></ol></aside>

    <article class="legal-card">
        <p class="legal-kicker">DashLane Mobility</p>
        <h1 class="legal-title">@T("Security Whitepaper")</h1>
        <p class="legal-updated">@T("Last updated: November 10, 2026")</p>
        @if (IsFrench)
        {
            <p class="notice legal-lang-note">@T("This document is currently available in English only.")</p>
        }

        <p class="legal-lead">
            This whitepaper describes how DashLane protects member accounts and personal information today: the architecture, the controls in place,
            the third parties involved, and the limitations we haven't addressed yet. It describes only controls that are implemented.
        </p>

        <div class="legal-summary">
            <h2>Key controls</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><span>Passwords are stored as <strong>salted PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA512 hashes</strong> (100,000 iterations).</span></li>
                <li><span>Uploaded photos and ID documents live <strong>outside the public web root</strong> and are served only to their owner.</span></li>
                <li><span>Phone codes are generated and checked by <strong>Twilio Verify</strong>; DashLane never stores them.</span></li>
                <li><span>Every state-changing form is protected by <strong>anti-forgery tokens</strong>, and setup steps are enforced on the server.</span></li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>1. Scope</h2>
            <p>
                This document covers the DashLane web application: account sign-up and sign-in, the five-step onboarding and verification flow, and the
                public pages. The ride feed, booking, payments and member messaging are not built yet and are out of scope; this document will be updated
                before they launch.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>2. Architecture</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Application:</strong> an ASP.NET Core 8 MVC application rendering server-side Razor views.</li>
                <li><strong>Database:</strong> a SQLite database (<code>App_Data/dashlane.db</code>) accessed through Entity Framework Core.</li>
                <li><strong>File storage:</strong> member uploads are stored on the server under <code>App_Data/members/{id}/</code>, outside <code>wwwroot</code>, so they can never be requested as static files.</li>
                <li><strong>External services:</strong> Twilio Verify (SMS codes), ID Analyzer (document checks), Google (optional sign-in) and OpenStreetMap Nominatim (city search). All calls are made server-to-server over HTTPS.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>3. Authentication and sessions</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Passwords must be at least 8 characters with a number and a symbol, and are hashed with ASP.NET Core's <code>PasswordHasher</code> (PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA512, a random 128-bit salt and 100,000 iterations). Plain-text passwords are never stored or logged.</li>
                <li>Failed sign-ins return the same generic message whether or not the email exists, to avoid revealing which accounts are registered.</li>
                <li>Sessions use an HTTP-only authentication cookie (<code>dashlane.auth</code>) with sliding expiration. Persistent sessions last at most 14 days; otherwise the session ends with the browser.</li>
                <li>Google sign-in uses Google's official OAuth flow. The temporary <code>dashlane.external</code> cookie expires after 5 minutes. Accounts are linked by Google's stable account identifier.</li>
                <li>If a session refers to an account that no longer exists, the session is ended and the user is signed out.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>4. Authorization and access to member files</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>All onboarding pages and file endpoints require an authenticated session.</li>
                <li>Profile photos and ID documents are served only through controller actions that look up the <strong>signed-in member's own</strong> file; there is no URL that accepts another member's ID.</li>
                <li>Document responses are sent with <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code> so browsers and proxies don't keep copies.</li>
                <li>The order of setup steps is enforced on the server: a request for a later step, including a direct form submission, is redirected to the first unfinished step.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>5. Data protection</h2>
            <h3>In transit</h3>
            <p>Outside development, the application redirects HTTP to HTTPS and sends HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS) headers. Calls to Twilio, ID Analyzer, Google and OpenStreetMap use HTTPS.</p>
            <h3>At rest</h3>
            <p>Passwords are hashed as described above. The SQLite database and uploaded files are not encrypted by the application itself; they rely on the hosting environment's disk encryption and file-system permissions.</p>
            <h3>Minimisation</h3>
            <p>DashLane doesn't store verification codes, payment details, IP addresses or biometric data. Only the decision, a short summary and a transaction reference are kept from each ID check.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>6. Identity verification</h2>
            <h3>Phone (Twilio Verify)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Twilio generates the 6-digit code, delivers it by SMS, expires it after 10 minutes, limits wrong attempts and limits how often codes can be sent to a number.</li>
                <li>DashLane only records the normalised phone number and whether it has been verified.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Government ID (ID Analyzer)</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>Photo uploads (JPG, PNG, WebP) are sent to ID Analyzer's <code>/scan</code> API with the member's legal name and date of birth. The scan is restricted to Canadian documents of the selected type.</li>
                <li>A <em>reject</em> decision keeps the member on the Identity step until a better document is uploaded; <em>review</em> and <em>accept</em> let them continue.</li>
                <li>PDFs, and uploads made while the integration isn't configured, are kept for manual review and are never sent to the provider.</li>
                <li>There is no face matching or liveness check.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>7. Application security controls</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Cross-site request forgery:</strong> every form that changes data carries an anti-forgery token that is validated on the server.</li>
                <li><strong>Injection:</strong> database access goes through Entity Framework Core with parameterised queries; Razor HTML-encodes output by default.</li>
                <li><strong>Uploads:</strong> an allow-list of file extensions, a 10 MB size limit, and server-chosen file names (the name you upload is discarded). Resolved paths are checked so they can't escape the member's own folder.</li>
                <li><strong>Third-party scripts:</strong> the site loads no third-party JavaScript, fonts or trackers; all assets are served from the application itself.</li>
                <li><strong>Error handling:</strong> outside development, unhandled errors show a generic page with a request ID instead of technical details.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>8. Secrets management</h2>
            <p>
                API keys and OAuth secrets are read from environment variables, loaded locally from a <code>.env</code> file that is excluded from source control.
                No secrets are embedded in the code or in the pages sent to the browser. Keys should be rotated in the provider's console if a <code>.env</code> file or server is ever exposed.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>9. Third-party processors</h2>
            <div class="legal-table-wrap">
                <table class="legal-table">
                    <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Used for</th><th>Data received</th></tr></thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Twilio</td><td>SMS verification codes</td><td>Mobile number, entered code, language</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>ID Analyzer</td><td>Document authenticity and data cross-check</td><td>Document image, legal name, date of birth</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Google</td><td>Optional sign-in</td><td>OAuth request only; returns name, email and account ID</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>OpenStreetMap Nominatim</td><td>City suggestions</td><td>Typed search text, sent from the server</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>10. Known limitations</h2>
            <p>We list these openly so members and reviewers can judge the current level of protection.</p>
            <ul>
                <li>No rate limiting or account lockout on password sign-in yet.</li>
                <li>No second sign-in factor beyond the verified phone number, and no self-serve password reset.</li>
                <li>The database and uploads are not encrypted at the application level.</li>
                <li>Uploads are validated by extension and size; file contents are not scanned for malware.</li>
                <li>No audit log of administrative access, and no self-serve account deletion.</li>
                <li>No third-party security certification (such as SOC 2 or ISO/IEC 27001) has been obtained.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>11. Incident response</h2>
            <p>
                If we discover a breach of security safeguards involving personal information that creates a real risk of significant harm, we will contain it,
                notify affected members, report it to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada, and keep a record of the breach, as required under PIPEDA.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section">
            <h2>12. Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
            <p>
                If you believe you've found a security issue, please report it privately with steps to reproduce. Please don't access other members' data,
                degrade the service or publicly disclose the issue before we've had a reasonable chance to fix it.
            </p>
            <div class="legal-contact">
                @if (Site.HasSupportEmail)
                {
                    <span>Security contact: <a href="mailto:@Site.SupportEmail">@Site.SupportEmail</a></span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span>This DashLane site hasn't published a security contact address yet.</span>
                }
            </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="legal-nav" aria-label="Related policies">
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Privacy", "Home")">@T("Privacy Policy")</a>
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Terms", "Home")">@T("Terms of Service")</a>
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("CommunityStandards", "Home")">@T("Community Standards")</a>
            <a class="legal-nav-link" href="@Url.Action("Help", "Home")">@T("Help Centre")</a>
        </nav>
    </article>
</div>
